  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 243 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCool and Dry Sunday:\n\nA longwave mid-level trough will remain parked over the Eastern US \non Sunday, with the coolest airmass of the season thus far resulting \nin a widespread freeze across North and Central GA Sunday morning. \nFor context, this first freeze will fall right in line with the \naverage first freeze time period of mid-November for our area. Under \nsunny skies, temperatures will slowly climb into the mid to upper \n50s in North GA, and low 60s in Central GA.\n\nSlow Warming and Mostly Dry Next Week:\n\nFrom early to middle portions of next week, the longwave trough will \nget kicked off to the northeast as another wave slides from the \nSouthern Plains across the Southeast US. It's not looking like this \nsystem will have much moisture or punch to it, but could still bring \na slight chance (10-15%) of showers to south-central GA Monday night \ninto Tuesday. This wave will also moderate the temperatures a bit, \nwith lows climbing back above freezing, and highs returning to the \n60s. Behind this system, guidance continues to show a large ridge \nbuilding from the Gulf up across the Southeast US from mid to late \nnext week, which would keep conditions dry and bring warmer \ntemperatures back into the area. Highs will likely climb back into \nthe 70s by Friday, with overnight lows in the 40s to near 50.",
